  "headline": "An oil-driven inflation squeeze that markets are meeting with higher rates, not fear",
  "body": [
    "The dashboard reads as an energy shock being absorbed through the rates and dollar channel rather than through panic. Brent is up 80% year to date and the 10-year yield is up 95 basis points over the same stretch, while gold sits near the bottom of its 52-week range. That is the signature of markets pricing persistent inflation and a tighter policy response, not a flight to safety. Drop Site News names the cause plainly, reporting that Iran is forcing oil and inflation up and that Ansarallah has struck Aramco.",
    "The relationship that carries the argument is gold falling as yields rise. Gold is down 6% on the month while the dollar index is up 1.6%, which says investors are buying real yields and dollars rather than hedging catastrophe. Risk appetite is intact underneath: the copper/gold ratio is up 5% over the month and one bitcoin now buys 9% more gold than it did a month ago, a liquidity and risk signal rather than a fear one.",
    "The tension is at the long end. The 10-year has risen 49 basis points over three months against 30 for the 30-year, so the curve is flattening as yields climb, a shape that says the market expects the policy response to carry a growth cost. Copper's 2% weekly slip leans the same way. The inflation reading deserves more weight, because copper is still up 18% on the year and sits high in its range, hardly a growth collapse.",
    "On the supply side, The Electronic Intifada reports Yemen striking Saudi Arabia and Drop Site News reports oil at a five-month high. Brent's reaction is telling: barely changed today and up 3% on the week, with the price still well short of its 52-week high. Markets have priced a sustained Gulf risk premium but not an escalation. Continued attacks on Saudi infrastructure are bullish for Brent, a headwind for USD/CAD given Canada's exporter status, and a drag on the rupee as an importer's currency; they also keep upward pressure on Treasury yields. Drop Site's reference to Trump turning to Putin is a potential offset through the supply channel, but nothing in prices suggests it is being counted on.",
    "This week's pullback in yields, 10 basis points on the 10-year and a softer dollar, is a pause rather than a turn. Gold did not bounce on the dip in rates, which confirms the haven bid is absent and the dominant trade remains inflation pricing. The rupee's 1% gain on the month against the dollar is the one outlier worth watching, since it runs against the oil-importer logic."
  ],
